1. A method of controlling exhaust gas recirculation in a turbocharged internal combustion engine including an intake passage, an exhaust passage, a turbocharger, an exhaust gas recirculation passage connecting the exhaust passage to the intake passage, an EGR valve disposed in the exhaust gas recirculation passage, and a differential pressure generating valve configured to vary a pressure difference between an exhaust-side pressure at an inlet of the exhaust gas recirculation passage and an intake-side pressure at an outlet of the exhaust gas recirculation passage, the method comprising:

determining, by a controller, a target exhaust gas recirculation ratio based on an operating condition of the engine;

detecting or estimating an abnormal-combustion tendency of the engine;

setting an allowable exhaust gas recirculation ratio in accordance with the abnormal-combustion tendency, the allowable exhaust gas recirculation ratio being reduced when the abnormal-combustion tendency exceeds a predetermined threshold;

controlling an opening degree of the EGR valve and an opening degree of the differential pressure generating valve so as to generate a pressure difference for supplying recirculated exhaust gas to the intake passage and to make an actual exhaust gas recirculation ratio approach the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio when the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio is not greater than the allowable exhaust gas recirculation ratio; and

when the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio exceeds the allowable exhaust gas recirculation ratio, limiting the actual exhaust gas recirculation ratio to the allowable exhaust gas recirculation ratio by at least one of decreasing the opening degree of the EGR valve and changing the opening degree of the differential pressure generating valve,

wherein the controller coordinates the EGR valve and the differential pressure generating valve to maintain boost pressure generated by the turbocharger while suppressing abnormal combustion in the engine.

1. A low-pressure exhaust gas recirculation system for an internal combustion engine, comprising:  

   a turbo supercharger including a turbine disposed in an exhaust passage of the engine and a compressor disposed in an intake passage of the engine;  

   an exhaust gas recirculation passage configured to recirculate exhaust gas from the exhaust passage downstream of the turbine to the intake passage upstream of the compressor;  

   an exhaust gas recirculation amount control valve disposed in the exhaust gas recirculation passage and configured to regulate an amount of exhaust gas recirculated through the exhaust gas recirculation passage;  

   a differential pressure valve disposed in the intake passage at a position upstream of a connection point at which the exhaust gas recirculation passage is connected to the intake passage, the differential pressure valve being configured to generate a pressure difference for promoting flow of exhaust gas through the exhaust gas recirculation passage; and  

   a controller configured to control at least the exhaust gas recirculation amount control valve and the differential pressure valve,  

   wherein the controller is configured to determine a target exhaust gas recirculation ratio based on an operating condition of the engine and to change an actual exhaust gas recirculation ratio toward the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio at a limited rate of change that is set to maintain stable operation of the engine.

1. A method for controlling exhaust gas recirculation in a turbocharged engine, the turbocharged engine including an intake passage, an exhaust passage, a turbocharger having a compressor disposed in the intake passage and a turbine disposed in the exhaust passage, an exhaust gas recirculation passage connecting the exhaust passage to the intake passage, an exhaust gas recirculation valve disposed in the exhaust gas recirculation passage, an intake throttle valve disposed in the intake passage, and a controller configured to control openings of the exhaust gas recirculation valve and the intake throttle valve, the method comprising:

determining, by the controller, a target exhaust gas recirculation ratio for the turbocharged engine;

determining, by the controller, a current exhaust gas recirculation ratio of the turbocharged engine;

determining, by the controller, a rate of change for changing the current exhaust gas recirculation ratio toward the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio, the rate of change being limited to a rate that prevents abnormal combustion in the turbocharged engine; and

controlling, by the controller, both an opening of the exhaust gas recirculation valve and an opening of the intake throttle valve so that the exhaust gas recirculation ratio changes from the current exhaust gas recirculation ratio toward the target exhaust gas recirculation ratio at the limited rate of change.
